WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.410
Film Courage: What are some common mistakes
you see web series creators make over and

00:00:04.410 --> 00:00:05.410
over again?

00:00:05.410 --> 00:00:10.490
Kathie Fong Yoneda: I think one of the things
that a lot of people don’t understand exactly

00:00:10.490 --> 00:00:17.630
what a web series is sometimes what they are
trying to do is they’re trying to take maybe

00:00:17.630 --> 00:00:26.590
a script (a film script) maybe like 100 minutes
or 90 minutes and they’re trying to do is

00:00:26.590 --> 00:00:29.529
they’re trying to cut it up into episodes.

00:00:29.529 --> 00:00:32.440
It would be like this is my movie and I’m
trying to cut it up.

00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:44.250
So the problem is when this is shown web series
audiences are very fickle people and the main

00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:50.560
this is because we’ve got that little mouse
and we click on that thing anytime or television

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:53.130
set (we just click the remote) and change
channels.

00:00:53.130 --> 00:00:58.510
And you can click and go onto a another web
series very easily if you don’t catch someone’s

00:00:58.510 --> 00:01:00.330
attention right away.

00:01:00.330 --> 00:01:05.050
People don’t understand you can’t just
take a film script and chop it up and that’s

00:01:05.050 --> 00:01:06.050
it.

00:01:06.050 --> 00:01:09.710
You have 90 minutes or 100 minutes to tell
the whole story.

00:01:09.710 --> 00:01:16.710
You need to capture somebodies attention right
away within the first 2 or 3 minutes.

00:01:16.710 --> 00:01:22.810
Especially because I’m with the LA WebFest
(I’m on their board) and one of our common

00:01:22.810 --> 00:01:28.670
complaints when we get people sending things
in is they will have web series that will

00:01:28.670 --> 00:01:31.320
15, 20, 25 minutes long.

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:36.270
That’s not a web series, that’s a short
film.

00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:43.530
In fact a lot of the web fest people now we’re
trying to limit it because a web series is…because

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:48.640
we like to show them and we like to show as
many episodes as we can but we can’t if

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:53.730
someone’s got 25-minute episodes then it’s
taking up too much time.

00:01:53.730 --> 00:02:00.290
So we tell people a web series (if it’s
a comedy) it should be longer than 6 minutes.

00:02:00.290 --> 00:02:06.470
If it’s a drama, no more than 8-10 minutes
at the most per episode.

00:02:06.470 --> 00:02:16.660
So I think it’s understanding that web series
should be looked at as its own medium.

00:02:16.660 --> 00:02:21.790
Not just this is a movie and just cut it up
into little pieces.

00:02:21.790 --> 00:02:23.430
Film Courage: Interesting.

00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:27.560
And how many episodes would you recommend
most web series have?

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:32.980
Kathie: You know the average web series, I’ve
seen some that were as little as 4-5 episodes

00:02:32.980 --> 00:02:34.099
for one season.

00:02:34.099 --> 00:02:36.980
I would say the average is 8 to 10 a season.

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:46.420
And dramas tend to be slightly longer, as
I mentioned most of them are between 8-10

00:02:46.420 --> 00:02:50.280
minutes and the comedies are more like 4 to
6 minutes.

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:54.130
Some of them are as short as a minute and
a half to 2 minutes.

00:02:54.130 --> 00:03:00.790
Film Courage: What are your thoughts about
a web series creator making a web series and

00:03:00.790 --> 00:03:03.569
aiming for sponsorship right away.

00:03:03.569 --> 00:03:06.780
I’m hearing this from a lot of web series
people “We’re looking for sponsorship.”

00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:09.400
And that’s like the only goal behind it.”

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:16.840
Kathie: Well if you don’t have a product,
you’re not going to get a sponsor.

00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:23.930
I mean it’s like the same thing as saying
“Okay, I’ve got to find a studio to distribute

00:03:23.930 --> 00:03:24.930
my movie.”

00:03:24.930 --> 00:03:27.200
Well, okay but you haven’t written the movie
yet.

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:34.330
So I think a lot of people get mislead into
thinking well if I can get this crowdfunding

00:03:34.330 --> 00:03:40.790
campaign going and money raised, then I can
do all of this.

00:03:40.790 --> 00:03:44.861
Most of the web series people that I’ve
talked to, they just started out because they

00:03:44.861 --> 00:03:48.070
thought they needed to do it and they wanted
to do it.

00:03:48.070 --> 00:03:49.209
And they had so much fun.

00:03:49.209 --> 00:03:55.819
It was just a bunch of people, a lot of times
they were cousins or brothers or siblings

00:03:55.819 --> 00:03:56.819
or something.

00:03:56.819 --> 00:04:03.940
A lot of times it was just kids in a dorm
that just decided “Let’s do one.”

00:04:03.940 --> 00:04:04.940
They just did it.

00:04:04.940 --> 00:04:09.790
And we’ve had some that have been so primitive
as just using their iPhone.

00:04:09.790 --> 00:04:15.140
And we’ve had some that have been very sophisticated
where it was actually wonderful technical

00:04:15.140 --> 00:04:17.049
things with cameras and stuff like that.

00:04:17.049 --> 00:04:20.959
And some have one brilliant special effects
and things like that.

00:04:20.959 --> 00:04:24.750
It just depends on where you’re at and what
your story is.

00:04:24.750 --> 00:04:30.510
Some of the more successful stories though
are the ones where people just thought they

00:04:30.510 --> 00:04:38.309
needed to be done and they had an interesting
set of characters in an unusual circumstance.

00:04:38.309 --> 00:04:45.160
As an example…I love the stories that come
behind the web series and one of the things

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:51.189
that I’ve been doing is that I’ve been
asked to go to the different web fests around

00:04:51.189 --> 00:04:56.919
the world talking about web series that concern
social issues.

00:04:56.919 --> 00:05:07.439
And it’s very interesting because one of
them is called Fasciteene because it involves

00:05:07.439 --> 00:05:12.940
a situation that is in Lebanon (Beirut, Lebanon).

00:05:12.940 --> 00:05:25.789
The creator of it, her name is Katia and she
grew up in Lebanon…a middle class family

00:05:25.789 --> 00:05:29.590
or upper middle class (I don’t know) but
they own some apartments.

00:05:29.590 --> 00:05:38.220
And she did her college work in London because
there weren’t any Universities that she

00:05:38.220 --> 00:05:46.770
felt she could go to in Lebanon that would
support the fact that she wanted to do communication

00:05:46.770 --> 00:05:49.089
and television and stuff like that.

00:05:49.089 --> 00:05:52.199
And anyway her family sent her up there.

00:05:52.199 --> 00:05:54.479
She actually did quite well.

00:05:54.479 --> 00:06:00.710
She started working for the BBC and she was
their Aljazeera contact because she could

00:06:00.710 --> 00:06:07.770
speak in several different languages and she
even got some awards but her parents were

00:06:07.770 --> 00:06:14.939
getting older so she made the decision to
move back to Beirut and stay with her family

00:06:14.939 --> 00:06:19.029
for awhile and see how things were there.

00:06:19.029 --> 00:06:24.610
And she realized that when she met with some
of her friends there that a lot of things

00:06:24.610 --> 00:06:27.909
had gotten worse in Lebanon.

00:06:27.909 --> 00:06:37.129
They had had some bombings and there were
areas (neighborhoods) where it used to be

00:06:37.129 --> 00:06:40.270
safe and it was no longer safe.

00:06:40.270 --> 00:06:46.719
She realized that there was something that
she wanted to say and she felt that women

00:06:46.719 --> 00:06:53.639
(especially) were the ones who were not progressing.

00:06:53.639 --> 00:07:00.669
Fascateen is about three women who eventually
become best friends.

00:07:00.669 --> 00:07:07.559
And one is where her husband has left her
and their kid at his parent’s place.

00:07:07.559 --> 00:07:09.039
He is through with marriage.

00:07:09.039 --> 00:07:10.199
He doesn’t want to be married.

00:07:10.199 --> 00:07:13.680
He doesn’t want to be a father.

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:20.499
And she is left by herself with their young
child with the in-laws.

00:07:20.499 --> 00:07:22.279
And she doesn’t know what she should do.

00:07:22.279 --> 00:07:23.830
She does not get along with the in-laws.

00:07:23.830 --> 00:07:26.250
They blame her for everything wrong.

00:07:26.250 --> 00:07:30.499
“Our son wouldn’t have left if you were
a good wife or a good mother.”

00:07:30.499 --> 00:07:32.240
They constantly blame her.

00:07:32.240 --> 00:07:35.110
So she needs to find a place to stay.

00:07:35.110 --> 00:07:43.460
She ends up finding a place to stay in an
apartment building and the daughter of the

00:07:43.460 --> 00:07:51.729
apartment owner and she is very creative and
she likes to make movies and she’s just

00:07:51.729 --> 00:07:56.520
curious about things and she writes and she
does poetry and she’s just very creative.

00:07:56.520 --> 00:07:58.909
And the father keeps saying “When are you
going to settle down and get married?

00:07:58.909 --> 00:08:00.499
I ought to just marry you off!”

00:08:00.499 --> 00:08:03.189
And she doesn’t want that to happen.

00:08:03.189 --> 00:08:08.120
And the father though is…her parents are
very well-meaning but they’re very old fashioned

00:08:08.120 --> 00:08:09.310
about it.

00:08:09.310 --> 00:08:17.039
But she sweet talks her father into accepting
this woman into being in one of their apartments.

00:08:17.039 --> 00:08:27.800
The woman has to work so she takes the son
and she’s a 

00:08:27.800 --> 00:08:34.710
tutor so she tutors for this woman who is
very wealthy and she has a couple of kids

00:08:34.710 --> 00:08:42.419
and her husband works in Dubai and so she’s
by herself and so she’s got a Nanny and

00:08:42.419 --> 00:08:48.280
she’s got all of this but she needs a tutor
so the woman who is divorced is the tutor.

00:08:48.280 --> 00:08:51.930
But it’s this three women who actually become
very good friends.

00:08:51.930 --> 00:08:58.000
And what she did at the end of each web series
was they got to vote on the ending.

00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:00.210
Film Courage: The audience did?

00:09:00.210 --> 00:09:05.970
Kathie: They got to vote on the ending and
then they would show the ending at the beginning

00:09:05.970 --> 00:09:11.420
of the next episode (whichever one got to
vote) and I asked her “Well why did you

00:09:11.420 --> 00:09:12.420
do that?”

00:09:12.420 --> 00:09:19.970
And she said “I wanted to give women the
opportunity to vote, to have an option.

00:09:19.970 --> 00:09:23.980
Because they have so few options in my country.”

00:09:23.980 --> 00:09:30.450
And so Fasateen won the grand prize at the
Marseille web fest.

00:09:30.450 --> 00:09:31.450
Film Courage: Wow.

00:09:31.450 --> 00:09:32.450
Good for her.

00:09:32.450 --> 00:09:34.390
And I’m sorry, what was the name of the
web series?

00:09:34.390 --> 00:09:39.030
Kathie: Fasateen and it’s an amazing one.

